schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: "Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)"
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    An AI answers only from documents you give it instead of its general
    training, and returns citations back to the exact passage it drew from.
  source_traditions:
    - information-retrieval + language-model synthesis (NLP research)
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Named in the article as "the single most useful pattern for a knowledge
    commons, because answers come with receipts." Google NotebookLM is given
    as the accessible entry point: load your governance docs and meeting
    summaries, then a new member can ask "how do we make spending decisions?"
    and get a sourced answer instead of pinging a person on Discord.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - general-purpose chat with an LLM's training-data knowledge (no grounding, no citations)
    - fine-tuning (baking documents into model weights, a different technique with different risks)
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      "when it cites nothing, the knowledge is not there" — the article frames
      an uncited answer as a documentation gap to fill, not a failure of the
      tool.
  tensions:
    - >-
      grounded-but-cloud-hosted (NotebookLM, convenient, but files live on
      Google's servers) vs local-and-private (Smart Connections on Obsidian,
      slower to adopt, fully offline).
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Treating RAG as "AI search" flattens the distinction the article leans
    on: the discipline of the format (a system that literally cannot answer
    beyond what's uploaded) is what makes it trustworthy for governance
    questions, not any general capability of the underlying model.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/ai-community-tools.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-ae4676958221
  source_lineage: ai-community-tools.md
